1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,140
Imagine you wake up tomorrow, walk into the bathroom, and there's no mirror.

2
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:05,260
Not broken.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:06,320
Not missing.

4
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:08,020
It never existed.

5
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:10,800
Nobody in history ever invented one.

6
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:15,540
So you fix your hair by touch, and you have absolutely no idea what it looks

7
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:15,820
like.

8
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:17,360
Neither does anyone else.

9
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:18,620
Look at this street.

10
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:22,760
Everyone looks like they lost a fight with a hedge, and nobody minds, because

11
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:24,600
nobody has seen themselves in years.

12
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:25,780
Except Gary.

13
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:27,900
Gary has never seen himself either.

14
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:29,940
Gary believes he is an eagle.

15
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:34,000
By the end of this video you'll find out why a world without mirrors would have

16
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,860
no space telescopes, much stranger dating, why the face you think is yours might

17
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:42,400
not be the one everyone else likes, and why the people in this world might

18
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,000
actually be happier than you.

19
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:45,120
Room 1.

20
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:46,220
The rewind.

21
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,180
To understand how big this is, rewind about 8,000 years.

22
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:54,680
Some of the oldest mirrors ever found were made from polished obsidian, volcanic

23
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:58,120
glass, in what is now Turkey, at a site called Çatalhöyük.

24
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,920
Before that, the only way to see your own face was a still pond.

25
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,680
In this world, nobody polishes that rock.

26
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:09,140
So for all of human history, your face is something other people see, and you

27
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,060
basically don't.

28
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:13,940
For most of history after that, good mirrors were rare and expensive.

29
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:18,700
By the 1500s the best ones came from glassmakers on the island of Murano, near

30
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:18,980
Venice,

31
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:23,460
and Venice guarded the secret so closely that its glassworkers were barred from

32
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:23,760
leaving.

33
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,940
When King Louis the Fourteenth of France wanted to show off, he built the Hall

34
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:30,370
of Mirrors at Versailles.

35
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:32,400
357 mirrors in one room.

36
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:33,840
It was a flex.

37
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:39,280
It said, I'm so rich, I can look at myself 357 times at once.

38
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:43,480
In this world, Louis gets 357 blank panels.

39
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,560
He has to find another way to show off.

40
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:46,880
He chooses hats.

41
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:52,400
Then in 1835, a German chemist named Justus von Liebig works out how to coat

42
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,160
glass with a thin layer of silver.

43
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:56,720
That's the cheap, clear mirror in your bathroom today.

44
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:58,880
In this world, he goes home early.

45
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:00,680
Your bathroom wall stays blank.

46
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:02,240
Which sounds fine.

47
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,020
Until tomorrow morning, when you have to get ready.

48
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,120
Room 2.

49
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:07,080
Getting ready.

50
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:10,300
Without mirrors, getting ready becomes a team sport.

51
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:11,880
Couples do each other's makeup.

52
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,080
Roommates check each other's teeth for spinach.

53
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:15,700
Do I have something on my face?

54
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,220
is the most common question on Earth.

55
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,460
Getting a haircut becomes an act of pure trust.

56
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:25,880
You walk out, you can't see it, and you just have to believe your barber when he

57
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:27,560
says, Trust me. It's great.

58
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:29,180
It does not look great.

59
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:30,900
Everyone says it looks great.

60
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,680
Society runs on this lie.

61
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:33,800
Gary agrees.

62
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:35,560
Gary looks majestic.

63
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:37,900
Clothes shopping changes completely.

64
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,700
There's no mirror in the fitting room, so every store hires professional

65
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:42,380
describers.

66
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:44,300
The jacket says confidence.

67
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:47,300
The pants say... we'll come back to the pants.

68
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:49,780
Shopping alone becomes impossible.

69
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:53,580
You need at least two friends, and they need to be honest friends, which is

70
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:55,000
harder to find than a good jacket.

71
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:59,080
The beauty industry still exists, but it has to sell you something you'll never

72
00:02:59,080 --> 00:02:59,940
see on yourself.

73
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:02,820
Lipstick is sold on how it makes other people react.

74
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:04,860
Ads don't say look your best.

75
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:07,040
They say your friends will gasp.

76
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:09,280
And gyms have no mirror walls.

77
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:12,980
So nobody can check their form, and nobody can take a mirror selfie.

78
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:16,980
Honestly, that's two problems solved and one created.

79
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:18,300
Room 3.

80
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,200
Your home feels smaller.

81
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:22,940
Interior designers lose their favorite trick.

82
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:27,040
In our world, a big mirror on the wall makes a tiny apartment look twice the

83
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:27,400
size.

84
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:30,340
Without it, your tiny apartment is just tiny.

85
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,920
Dance studios have no mirror walls either.

86
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:36,560
Dancers learn by watching each other, and by having a teacher yell at them.

87
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:38,820
To be fair, the yelling already happens.

88
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:41,820
But the place you'd miss mirrors most isn't your apartment.

89
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:43,500
It's your love life.

90
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:44,860
Room 4.

91
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:45,560
Dating.

92
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,620
Dating gets very strange.

93
00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:49,680
You can't check yourself before you go.

94
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:53,180
So the first time you find out how you look tonight is from your date's face

95
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:53,980
when you walk in.

96
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:55,160
Was that a good face?

97
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:56,180
A bad face?

98
00:03:56,540 --> 00:03:59,320
There's ketchup on your forehead, and you will never know.

99
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:02,700
But here's where it gets weird, and this part is real science.

100
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:06,480
Every time you look in a mirror, you see your face flipped left to right.

101
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:08,680
Nobody else ever sees that version.

102
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:09,960
They see the real one.

103
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:14,300
In 1977, researchers showed people two photos of their own face:

104
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,440
the true one, and a mirror-flipped one.

105
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:19,820
People preferred the flipped one, the version they're used to seeing.

106
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:23,240
Their friends preferred the true one, the version they're used to seeing.

107
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:25,420
That's called the mere-exposure effect.

108
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,200
We like what we're familiar with.

109
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:31,460
So in our world, part of the reason you think you look odd in photos is that

110
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:34,280
you've spent your life staring at a reversed copy of yourself.

111
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:37,200
In a world without mirrors, that never happens.

112
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:40,820
The only face you know is the one your friends describe to you.

113
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:43,980
So how would you ever find out what you really look like?

114
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,300
There's exactly one way left.

115
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:47,580
Room 5.

116
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:49,620
Photographs become power.

117
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:52,100
Photographs still exist in this world.

118
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:56,660
That makes them the only way to really see yourself. Which makes photographers

119
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:58,600
the most powerful people alive.

120
00:04:59,220 --> 00:04:59,600
Fun fact:

121
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:04,180
in 1839, an American named Robert Cornelius took a photo of himself.

122
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:08,720
It's the oldest surviving portrait photograph made in America, and people call

123
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:09,800
it the first selfie.

124
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:12,240
In our world, it's a nice bit of history.

125
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,780
In this world, he'd be a celebrity.

126
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:18,660
He saw his own face before almost anyone else alive.

127
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,680
Front-facing phone cameras would be the biggest product launch in history.

128
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,840
People would cry seeing their faces for the first time.

129
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:27,080
Some would scream.

130
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:30,100
Gary sees himself on a phone for the first time.

131
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:31,640
He is not an eagle.

132
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:33,080
He takes it badly.

133
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:36,500
Before phones, photo booths would be treated like temples.

134
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:40,320
People would save up for a yearly portrait to find out how they'd aged.

135
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:43,180
Imagine finding out what you look like once a year.

136
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:44,760
Oh. I have a mole.

137
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:46,120
When did I get a mole?

138
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:48,000
Dating apps get strict.

139
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:53,020
A photo is the only proof of what anyone looks like, so every profile has to

140
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,560
show when each picture was taken.

141
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,240
A photo more than a year old counts as lying.

142
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,120
This guy hasn't updated his since 2009.

143
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:02,460
He has no idea he's gone bald.

144
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,640
Nobody has had the heart to tell him.

145
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:07,420
And beauty filters would make no sense at all.

146
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:11,100
In our world, a filter edits a face you've seen a million times.

147
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:15,600
In this world, the first version of your face you ever see might already be

148
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:15,960
filtered.

149
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:18,960
You might honestly not know which one is the real you.

150
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:20,500
That's not what I look like.

151
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:22,280
That's exactly what you look like.

152
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:24,120
That's what you've looked like for 20 years.

153
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:25,740
We just didn't want to tell you.

154
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,740
But your face is the small stuff.

155
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:32,560
Without mirrors, some of the biggest things humans ever built stop working.

156
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:33,640
Room 6.

157
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:35,280
The big stuff breaks.

158
00:06:35,790 --> 00:06:37,700
Now for the stuff you don't think about.

159
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:39,940
Cars have no rearview mirrors.

160
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:43,380
Changing lanes means turning your whole body around and hoping.

161
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:45,220
Every highway looks like this.

162
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:46,900
Science takes a hit too.

163
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:51,580
In 1668, Isaac Newton built one of the first reflecting telescopes.

164
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:55,000
It used a curved mirror instead of a lens to gather light.

165
00:06:55,420 --> 00:07:00,020
Today the James Webb Space Telescope collects light with 18 gold-coated mirror

166
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:00,560
segments.

167
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,060
That's how we've photographed galaxies billions of light-years away.

168
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:06,280
In this world, it never launches.

169
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:09,040
Our view of the universe stays much blurrier.

170
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:12,140
And lasers work by bouncing light between mirrors.

171
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:13,580
So no lasers.

172
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:15,180
No laser eye surgery.

173
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,440
No laser barcode scanners at the checkout.

174
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:19,440
And cats have nothing to chase.

175
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:22,660
Your dentist loses the tiny mirror on a stick.

176
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:27,290
So now every checkup is just them squinting into your mouth and going, Hmm.

177
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:31,360
The simple periscope, a tube with two mirrors in it, never exists.

178
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:34,660
Real submarine periscopes use glass prisms instead,

179
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:36,080
so the Navy is fine.

180
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:38,420
Every science-fair project is not.

181
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:41,020
And those round safety mirrors on blind corners?

182
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:41,740
Gone.

183
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:44,400
Every parking garage becomes a game of chicken.

184
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:45,580
Room 7.

185
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:46,540
The twist.

186
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:49,420
So is a world without mirrors just worse?

187
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:50,620
Maybe not.

188
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:55,080
In 1970, a psychologist named Gordon Gallup put a mirror in front of

189
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:55,420
chimpanzees.

190
00:07:56,140 --> 00:07:59,620
After several days, they started using it to look at parts of themselves they

191
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:00,580
couldn't normally see.

192
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:04,040
That became the famous mirror test for self-recognition.

193
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:08,400
Human babies usually start to pass it somewhere between 18 months and two years

194
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:08,720
old.

195
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:11,020
That's the moment you realize, That's me.

196
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:14,920
And not long after, you start worrying about what me looks like.

197
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:19,020
In a world without mirrors, that moment still comes, because there are puddles

198
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:19,580
and photos.

199
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:20,880
But it comes rarely.

200
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:24,320
You'd spend most of your life looking outward instead of at yourself.

201
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:26,840
You'd know your friends' faces better than your own.

202
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:30,660
You'd judge people on how they act, because that's what you can actually see.

203
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:33,540
Gary has decided he doesn't care what the phone said.

204
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,120
His friends call him an eagle.

205
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,360
He's going with that.

206
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:40,420
So tomorrow, when you look in the mirror and decide you look a little off,

207
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:41,460
remember two things.

208
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:43,980
One: you're looking at the flipped version.

209
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:47,600
Your friends see a different face, and it's the one they actually like.

210
00:08:48,020 --> 00:08:52,000
Two: there's a pigeon out there who's never seen himself, and he's having the

211
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
best life of anyone in this video.

212
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,880
Would you rather live in a world without mirrors, or a world where you can see

213
00:08:57,880 --> 00:08:59,960
exactly what everyone thinks you look like?

214
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:01,040
Tell me in the comments.

